  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「ケイアップル」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%82%B1%E3%82%A4%E3%82%A2%E3%83%83%E3%83%97%E3%83%AB",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 106360572,
      "text": "ケイアップル は、南アフリカ原産の常緑中低木。ヤナギ科に属す（従来の分類ではイイギリ科とされていた）。名称の由来であるグレイト・ケイ川流域からアフリカ大陸東海岸沿いの北はタンザニアまでが産地である。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Kei apple」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kei_apple",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1371400417,
      "text": "Dovyalis caffra, commonly known as the Kei apple, is a small to medium-sized tree, native to southern Africa. Its distribution extends from the Kei River in the south, from which the common name derives, northwards along the eastern side of the continent to Tanzania. The ripe fruits are edible and similar in appearance to apricots, though they are quite acidic due to having a high concentration of malic acid."
    }
  },
